{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00420 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Promazine, a centrally acting antipsychotic, is linked to Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways involving allergic and inflammatory conditions. The evidence suggests that Promazine may exert a modulatory effect on the immune system, potentially triggering or exacerbating conditions like Atopic Conjunctivitis and Olopatadine, which in turn are associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis. The shared pathway from Promazine to Vasomotor Rhinitis involves the induction of allergic responses, with Promazine possibly acting as a mediator in the progression of these conditions. The cumulative evidence points to a mechanism where Promazine influences inflammatory pathways, leading to the development or worsening of Vasomotor Rhinitis. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Promazine in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through its interaction with allergic and inflammatory processes.",
